# SRG-APP-000141-WSR-000076  -  CAT II
# The web server must not be a proxy server.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000141-WSR-000076
group:           V-206376
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-000381
control_rev5:    CM-7

contribution:    Splunk Enterprise ships no forward- or reverse-proxy service for
                 third-party traffic, so there is no proxy function to uninstall; the one
                 relay it does offer is the app-browse channel to the remote applications
                 repository, and the application can close it.

governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.

check:           server.conf [applicationsManagement] allowInternetAccess in false|0
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          server.conf [applicationsManagement] allowInternetAccess https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Any reverse proxy, load balancer, or egress proxy placed in front of or
                 beside the instance, carried by that network component rather than by the
                 application; web.conf tools.proxy.on only tells Splunk that such a proxy
                 is present so it can read the forwarded headers, and server.conf
                 [proxyConfig] only tells splunkd to send its own outbound requests through
                 one - neither makes Splunk a proxy for other hosts (web.conf.spec:1016,
                 server.conf.spec:1201).
external:        web.conf enable_proxy_write governs the /splunkd endpoint that Splunk
                 Web's own interface uses to reach the daemon; it is internal to the
                 product's user interface, not a proxy for third-party traffic, and setting
                 it false breaks the interface and the client SDKs (web.conf.spec:371).

citation:        Web Server SRG SRG-APP-000141-WSR-000076 check and fix text;
                 server.conf.spec:1734 (allowInternetAccess), server.conf.spec:1201
                 ([proxyConfig] is an outbound client setting), web.conf.spec:1016
                 (tools.proxy.on describes being behind a reverse proxy, default false)
finding:         The instance is permitted to reach the remote applications repository, so
                 the application still relays user-initiated requests to an outside service
                 on the user's behalf.
